topics.body:
## Know it

`var` is the original way to declare a variable in JavaScript. It is function-scoped, can be redeclared, and is hoisted to the top of its function with the initial value `undefined`.

---

## Explanation

A `var` declared inside an `if` block or a loop is still visible in the whole function, because `var` ignores block boundaries. Declared outside any function, it becomes a global variable. These rules cause surprising bugs, which is why modern code prefers `let` and `const`.

## Example

```ts
function demo() {
  if (true) {
    var message = 'hello';
  }
  console.log(message); // 'hello', var leaks out of the block
}
demo();
```

## When to use it

Rarely. You mostly meet `var` in older code; new code should use `const` or `let`.

## Know it

`let` declares a block-scoped variable that can be reassigned. It cannot be redeclared in the same scope and it cannot be used before its declaration line.

---

## Explanation

Block-scoped means the variable exists only inside the nearest `{ }` block, such as an `if` statement or a loop body. A `let` variable is hoisted but stays in the temporal dead zone until the declaration runs, so reading it earlier throws a `ReferenceError`.

## Example

```ts
let count = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  count += i;
}
console.log(count); // 3
// console.log(i);  // ReferenceError, i only exists inside the loop
```

## When to use it

Use `let` when the value genuinely has to change, such as counters, accumulators, or a variable assigned in different branches.

## Know it

Function scope means a variable declared inside a function is available everywhere in that function and nowhere outside it. In JavaScript, `var` follows function scope.

---

## Explanation

Each function call creates its own scope, so variables with the same name in two different functions do not clash. Inside the function, a `var` is visible even before and outside the block where you wrote it.

## Example

```ts
function counter() {
  var total = 0;
  for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    total += i;
  }
  console.log(i);     // 3, i is visible in the whole function
  return total;
}
counter();
```

## When to use it

Function scope is useful to keep helper variables private to one function. Be careful with `var`, which leaks out of blocks.

## Know it

Block scope means a variable is only available inside the block `{ }` in which it is declared, such as an `if`, `for`, `while`, or a plain block. `let` and `const` are block-scoped.

---

## Explanation

When the block ends, the variables declared inside it are no longer reachable. This keeps temporary values from leaking into the surrounding code and makes loops safer, because every iteration of a `let` loop gets its own copy of the loop variable.

## Example

```ts
if (true) {
  let secret = 42;
  const label = 'answer';
}
// console.log(secret); // ReferenceError, it only exists inside the block
console.log(typeof secret); // 'undefined'
```

## When to use it

Use block scope by default with `let` and `const`, so each variable lives only as long as it is needed.

## Know it

A closure happens when a function retains access to variables from the scope where it was created, even after the outer function has finished. Closures are useful for maintaining state and creating private data.

---

## Explanation

A callback can still read variables from its outer function because of closure behavior.

## Example

```ts
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => ++count;
}
const next = makeCounter();
next(); // 1
next(); // 2
```

## Know it

`bind()` does not call the function. It returns a new function whose `this` value is permanently fixed, and it can also pre-fill some arguments.

---

## Explanation

Because the new function remembers its `this`, you can pass it as a callback without losing context. This used to be common in class components and event handlers. Arrow functions now solve most of the same problems.

## Example

```ts
const counter = {
  count: 5,
  show() { return this.count; }
};
const detached = counter.show.bind(counter);
console.log(detached()); // 5, still works when called on its own
```

## When to use it

Use it to pass a method as a callback while keeping the correct `this`, or to create a partially applied function.

## Know it

A class is the modern syntax for defining a blueprint for objects, with a `constructor`, methods, and inheritance through `extends`. Under the hood it is built on prototypes.

---

## Explanation

Class methods are placed on the prototype and shared by all instances. A class must be called with `new`, and class declarations are not usable before they are defined. Classes support `static` members, getters and setters, and private fields with `#`.

## Example

```ts
class Animal {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  speak() { return this.name + ' makes a sound'; }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() { return super.speak() + ': woof'; }
}
console.log(new Dog('Rex').speak());
```

## When to use it

Use classes when you need several objects with the same shape and behavior, or when you want inheritance.

## Know it

A constructor function is a regular function used with `new` to create objects. By convention its name starts with a capital letter, and it sets up the new object through `this`.

---

## Explanation

Methods are attached to `Constructor.prototype` so that all instances share them. Calling the function without `new` is a common mistake and can set properties on the wrong `this`. Classes are the modern, clearer way to write the same pattern.

## Example

```ts
function Person(this: any, name: string) {
  this.name = name;
}
Person.prototype.hello = function () { return 'Hi ' + this.name; };
const p = new (Person as any)('Aniket');
console.log(p.hello());
```

## When to use it

You mostly see it in older code. In new code prefer a class.

## Know it

`reduce()` folds an array into a single value by running a function that combines an accumulator with each item. The result can be a number, string, object, or even another array.

---

## Explanation

You give `reduce()` a callback `(accumulator, item) => newAccumulator` and an initial value. Always provide the initial value; without it the first item is used and an empty array throws an error. It is the most general array method, since `map()` and `filter()` can be written with it.

## Example

```ts
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const sum = nums.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
console.log(sum);  // 10
const counts = ['a', 'b', 'a'].reduce<Record<string, number>>((acc, k) => {
  acc[k] = (acc[k] || 0) + 1; return acc;
}, {});
console.log(counts);  // { a: 2, b: 1 }
```

## When to use it

Use it to total numbers, group items, build a lookup object, or any time the output is not an array of the same length.

## Know it

`event.preventDefault()` stops the browser's default action for an event, for example following a link or submitting a form.

---

## Explanation

The event still travels through the DOM and other listeners still run; only the built-in behavior is cancelled. It only works if the event is cancelable.

## Example

```ts
// A link normally navigates when clicked. preventDefault() cancels that.
const link = document.createElement('a');
link.href = '#next-page';
link.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();          // stop the browser's default action
});
const click = new MouseEvent('click', { bubbles: true, cancelable: true });
link.dispatchEvent(click);
console.log(click.defaultPrevented); // true, the navigation was cancelled
```

## When to use it

Use it to handle a form submit or link click yourself, for example with an API call.

## Know it

`event.stopPropagation()` stops the event from moving on to parent (or child) elements, so ancestor listeners do not run. The default browser action still happens.

---

## Explanation

It affects event bubbling and capturing. Use it carefully: stopping propagation can break other code, such as analytics or click-outside handlers, that relies on seeing the event. `stopImmediatePropagation()` also stops other listeners on the same element.

## Example

```ts
// A button inside a card. Clicking the button normally also "clicks" the card.
const card = document.createElement('div');
const button = document.createElement('button');
card.appendChild(button);
card.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('card clicked'));
button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.stopPropagation();         // the event stops here, it does not bubble up
  console.log('button clicked');
});
button.dispatchEvent(new Event('click', { bubbles: true }));
// prints only: button clicked   (the card listener never runs)
```

## When to use it

Use it when a click on an inner element must not trigger the handler of its container, such as a button inside a clickable card.

## Know it

Asynchronous code starts a task and lets the rest of the program continue; the result is handled later when the task finishes. Typical examples are network requests, timers, and file operations.

---

## Explanation

JavaScript handles this with callbacks, Promises, and `async/await`. The event loop runs the completion handlers when the call stack is free, so the page stays responsive while waiting.

## Example

```ts
console.log('start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('timer finished'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('promise finished'));
console.log('end');
// start, end, promise finished, timer finished
```

## When to use it

Use it for anything that takes time or depends on something outside your code, such as API calls and user events.

## Know it

An `interface` describes the shape of an object: which properties it has and their types. Classes can implement it, and other interfaces can extend it.

---

## Explanation

Interfaces exist only at compile time and produce no JavaScript. Two interfaces with the same name in the same scope merge into one (declaration merging), which is useful for extending library types. They are the usual choice for describing objects and class contracts.

## Example

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email?: string;        // optional
}
interface Admin extends User {
  permissions: string[];
}
const admin: Admin = { id: 1, name: 'Aniket', permissions: ['all'] };
console.log(admin.name, admin.permissions);
```

## When to use it

Use it to describe object shapes, API models, and contracts that classes implement.

## Know it

`readonly` marks a property (or array) so it cannot be assigned to after it is initialized. It is checked by the compiler only.

---

## Explanation

`readonly` applies to properties and works on object members, class fields, and arrays (`readonly string[]`). It disappears in the compiled JavaScript, so it does not stop runtime changes the way `Object.freeze()` does. `Readonly<T>` makes every property of a type read-only.

## Example

```ts
interface Config {
  readonly apiUrl: string;
}
const config: Config = { apiUrl: '/api' };
// config.apiUrl = '/other';   // compile error: cannot assign to a read-only property
const days: readonly string[] = ['Mon', 'Tue'];
// days.push('Wed');           // compile error
console.log(config.apiUrl, days.length);
```

## When to use it

Use it for values that must not change after creation, such as configuration and IDs.

## Know it

`Pick<T, Keys>` builds a new type containing only the listed properties of `T`.

---

## Explanation

You list the keys you want to keep as a union of string literals. It is useful to create smaller types from a large model, for example only the fields needed for a list view. The new type stays in sync when the original changes.

## Example

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
}
type UserSummary = Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'>;
const summary: UserSummary = { id: 1, name: 'Aniket' };
console.log(summary);
```

## When to use it

Use it when you need only a few fields of a larger type.

## Know it

`Omit<T, Keys>` builds a new type containing every property of `T` except the listed ones.

---

## Explanation

It is the opposite of `Pick`. It is ideal for removing sensitive or irrelevant fields, such as a password, from a type. When new properties are added to `T`, they automatically appear in the result.

## Example

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  password: string;
}
type PublicUser = Omit<User, 'password'>;
const user: PublicUser = { id: 1, name: 'Aniket' };   // no password allowed
console.log(user);
```

## When to use it

Use it when you want almost everything from a type except a few fields.

## Know it

A union of string literals is a type that allows only a fixed set of exact strings, such as `'pending' | 'success' | 'failed'`.

---

## Explanation

It exists only at compile time and creates no extra JavaScript. Values are plain strings, which makes them easy to send in JSON. TypeScript warns about typos and, with a `switch`, can check that every case is handled.

## Example

```ts
type Status = 'pending' | 'success' | 'failed';
function label(status: Status): string {
  switch (status) {
    case 'pending': return 'Please wait';
    case 'success': return 'Done';
    case 'failed': return 'Try again';
  }
}
console.log(label('success'));   // 'Done'
// label('done');                // compile error: not part of the union
```

## When to use it

Use it for simple sets of allowed values, especially ones that come from or go to an API.

## Know it

A runtime error happens while the program is running, for example reading a property of `undefined` or calling something that is not a function. It throws an exception that can crash the code.

---

## Explanation

TypeScript cannot catch every runtime error, especially for data from outside, like an API response that has a different shape than declared. You handle runtime errors with `try/catch`, validation, and safe access such as optional chaining.

## Example

```ts
const response: any = { user: null };
try {
  console.log(response.user.name);          // TypeError at runtime
} catch (error) {
  console.log('caught:', (error as Error).message);
}
console.log(response.user?.name ?? 'no user'); // safe version
```

## When to use it

Always expect runtime errors for external data, and handle them with validation and `try/catch`.

## Know it

`extends` creates inheritance. A class that `extends` another class gets its properties and methods, and an interface that `extends` another interface includes its members. In generics, `extends` also sets a constraint.

---

## Explanation

A subclass can add or override methods and call the parent with `super`. A class can extend only one class. In a generic like `T extends { id: number }`, `extends` means "T must be at least this shape".

## Example

```ts
class Animal {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  speak() { return this.name + ' makes a sound'; }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() { return super.speak() + ': woof'; }
}
console.log(new Dog('Rex').speak());

function getId<T extends { id: number }>(item: T) { return item.id; }
console.log(getId({ id: 7, name: 'x' }));
```

## When to use it

Use it to reuse and specialize behavior from a parent class, or to constrain a generic type.

## Know it

`implements` makes a class promise to follow an interface. TypeScript checks that the class has every required member, but the class gets no code from the interface.

---

## Explanation

It is a compile-time contract only. A class can implement several interfaces at once. Because no behavior is inherited, you write every method yourself.

## Example

```ts
interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;
}
class ConsoleLogger implements Logger {
  log(message: string) { console.log('[log]', message); }
}
const logger: Logger = new ConsoleLogger();
logger.log('works');   // [log] works
```

## When to use it

Use it to guarantee that a class matches a contract, which makes classes easy to swap and test.

## Know it

Compile-time types are the type annotations, interfaces, and generics that the compiler checks while you write code. They are removed completely when the code is compiled to JavaScript.

---

## Explanation

Because types are erased, they cannot be inspected at runtime: you cannot use `instanceof` with an interface or loop over a type. They give editor help and catch mistakes early, but they cannot validate data that arrives while the program runs.

## Example

```ts
interface User { id: number; name: string }
function show(user: User) { return user.name; }
// After compiling, the interface and the ': User' annotation disappear:
//   function show(user) { return user.name; }
console.log(show({ id: 1, name: 'Aniket' }));
// console.log(user instanceof User); // not possible, User is only a type
```

## When to use it

Use types to model your code and catch mistakes early, but remember they do not exist while the app runs.

## Know it

Runtime values are the actual data that exist while the program runs: numbers, strings, objects, arrays, and functions. Only these can be checked with code such as `typeof`, `instanceof`, or custom validation.

---

## Explanation

Data from an API, a form, or `JSON.parse()` is a runtime value, and TypeScript cannot guarantee its shape. To trust it you must validate it at runtime, for example with a type guard that checks the fields.

## Example

```ts
interface User { id: number; name: string }
function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return typeof value === 'object' && value !== null
    && typeof (value as any).id === 'number'
    && typeof (value as any).name === 'string';
}
const data: unknown = JSON.parse('{"id":1,"name":"Aniket"}');
console.log(isUser(data) ? 'valid user: ' + data.name : 'invalid');
console.log(isUser({ id: 'x' }));   // false
```

## When to use it

Use runtime checks for any data you do not control, and use types for code you do control.

## Know it

A component is the building block of an Angular user interface. It is a class decorated with `@Component` that controls one part of the screen through a template, styles, and a selector.

---

## Explanation

Each component has a TypeScript class (data and behavior) and an HTML template (what is shown). Components are arranged in a tree, receive data through `@Input`, and send events with `@Output`. Anything related to showing data or reacting to user actions belongs here.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-card',
  standalone: true,
  template: `<h3>{{ name }}</h3><button (click)="greet()">Say hi</button>`
})
export class UserCardComponent {
  @Input() name = '';
  greet() { console.log('Hi ' + this.name); }
}

<!-- used in another template -->
<app-user-card name="Aniket"></app-user-card>
```

## When to use it

Create a component for every piece of UI that has its own template and behavior, such as a card, a form, or a page.

## Know it

A service is a plain class that holds logic or data that is not tied to one screen, such as calling an API, sharing state, or logging. It is usually marked `@Injectable` and provided through dependency injection.

---

## Explanation

Services have no template. Because Angular injects them, many components can share the same instance, and tests can replace them easily. Keeping HTTP calls and business rules in services keeps components small.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class UserService {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  getUsers() {
    return this.http.get<User[]>('/api/users');
  }
}

// a component asks for it
export class UserListComponent {
  private users = inject(UserService);
  users$ = this.users.getUsers();
}
```

## When to use it

Use a service for API calls, shared state, and any logic that several components need.

## Know it

`providedIn: 'root'` registers a service in the root injector, so the whole application shares one single instance (a singleton). It is also tree-shakable: if nothing uses the service, it is left out of the bundle.

---

## Explanation

Any component or service can inject it, and state held in it is shared everywhere and lives as long as the app runs. This is the default choice for most services.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class AuthService {
  isLoggedIn = false;
}

// Two different components get the SAME instance:
// A sets authService.isLoggedIn = true, B sees true.
```

## When to use it

Use it for app-wide services such as authentication, configuration, and API clients.

## Know it

Component-level providers are listed in the `providers` array of a component. Angular creates a new instance of the service for each instance of that component, and destroys it with the component.

---

## Explanation

The instance is shared with the component's children but not with the rest of the app. This gives every component its own isolated state, for example a separate form or filter state for each copy of a widget.

## Example

```text
@Injectable()
export class CartState { items: string[] = []; }

@Component({
  selector: 'app-cart',
  standalone: true,
  providers: [CartState],     // a new CartState for every <app-cart>
  template: `...`
})
export class CartComponent {
  state = inject(CartState);
}
```

## When to use it

Use it when each component instance needs its own private service state.

## Know it

The constructor is the normal class constructor of the component. In Angular it is mainly used for dependency injection and very simple field setup. It runs when the object is created.

---

## Explanation

At constructor time, `@Input` values have not been set yet, and the template is not rendered, so inputs are not available. Heavy work, HTTP calls, and logic that depends on inputs should not go here. Modern code can also use the `inject()` function instead of constructor parameters.

## Example

```text
export class ProfileComponent {
  @Input() userId = 0;

  constructor(private users: UserService) {
    // OK: receive dependencies
    // NOT OK: this.userId is still 0 here, inputs are not set yet
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it only to receive dependencies and set simple default values.

## Know it

`ngOnInit` is a lifecycle hook that runs once, after Angular has set the component's first `@Input` values. It is the right place for initialization logic.

---

## Explanation

At this point inputs are available, so you can load data, set up subscriptions, and prepare the view. It runs only once per component instance, after the first `ngOnChanges`. Implement the `OnInit` interface to have the compiler check it.

## Example

```text
export class ProfileComponent implements OnInit {
  @Input() userId = 0;
  user$!: Observable<User>;

  ngOnInit() {
    // inputs are ready, so userId has its real value
    this.user$ = this.users.getUser(this.userId);
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it to load data and do start-up work that depends on inputs.

## Know it

`ngOnChanges` is a lifecycle hook that runs before `ngOnInit` and again every time one of the component's `@Input` bindings changes. It receives a `SimpleChanges` object with the previous and current values.

---

## Explanation

It only fires when the input reference changes. If a parent mutates an object in place, `ngOnChanges` is not triggered. It is useful when the component must react to a new input value, for example reloading data when an id changes.

## Example

```text
export class ProfileComponent implements OnChanges {
  @Input() userId = 0;

  ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges) {
    if (changes['userId']) {
      console.log('userId changed from',
        changes['userId'].previousValue, 'to', changes['userId'].currentValue);
      this.reload();
    }
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it to react whenever an input value changes after the first render.

## Know it

`ngAfterViewInit` runs once, after Angular has fully created the component's view and its child views. This is the first moment when `@ViewChild` references are available.

---

## Explanation

Use it when you need to touch the rendered DOM or a child component, for example to focus an input or start a chart library. Changing a bound value here can cause an `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenChecked` error.

## Example

```text
export class SearchComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChild('box') box!: ElementRef<HTMLInputElement>;

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    this.box.nativeElement.focus();   // the input exists now
  }
}
// template: <input #box>
```

## When to use it

Use it for one-time work that needs the rendered view, such as focusing an element or initializing a third-party widget.

## Know it

`ngAfterViewChecked` runs after every change detection pass of the component's view, including the first one. It runs very often.

---

## Explanation

Because it fires on nearly every interaction, heavy work in it makes the app slow, and changing bound state in it can cause errors. It is rarely needed; most one-time DOM work belongs in `ngAfterViewInit`.

## Example

```text
export class ChatComponent implements AfterViewChecked {
  @ViewChild('list') list!: ElementRef<HTMLElement>;

  ngAfterViewChecked() {
    // runs after EVERY check, so keep it tiny
    this.list.nativeElement.scrollTop = this.list.nativeElement.scrollHeight;
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it only for light work that must follow every view update, such as keeping a list scrolled to the bottom.

## Know it

`@Input()` lets a parent component pass data into a child component through property binding. Data flows down from parent to child.

---

## Explanation

The parent writes `[name]="value"` in its template, and the child declares `@Input() name`. When the bound value changes, the child receives the new value. Newer Angular also offers `input()` signal inputs.

## Example

```text
// child
@Component({ selector: 'app-badge', standalone: true, template: `<span>{{ label }}</span>` })
export class BadgeComponent {
  @Input() label = '';
}

<!-- parent template -->
<app-badge [label]="'New'"></app-badge>
```

## When to use it

Use it whenever a child needs data that belongs to its parent.

## Know it

`@Output()` lets a child component notify its parent about something that happened, using an `EventEmitter`. Events flow up from child to parent.

---

## Explanation

The child calls `emit()` with a value, and the parent listens with event binding `(eventName)="handler($event)"`. This keeps the child independent: it reports what happened but does not decide what to do.

## Example

```text
// child
@Component({
  selector: 'app-save-button',
  standalone: true,
  template: `<button (click)="saved.emit('record-1')">Save</button>`
})
export class SaveButtonComponent {
  @Output() saved = new EventEmitter<string>();
}

<!-- parent template -->
<app-save-button (saved)="onSaved($event)"></app-save-button>
```

## When to use it

Use it when a child must tell its parent about a click, a save, or a change.

## Know it

`@ViewChild` queries the component's own template and returns the first matching element, directive, or child component. It lets the class talk directly to something in its view.

---

## Explanation

You can query by a template reference such as `#box`, or by a component or directive class. The result is available from `ngAfterViewInit`. With `{ static: true }` it can be read earlier if the element is not inside an `*ngIf` or `@if`.

## Example

```text
export class FormComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChild('nameInput') nameInput!: ElementRef<HTMLInputElement>;

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    this.nameInput.nativeElement.focus();
  }
}
// template: <input #nameInput>
```

## When to use it

Use it to reach a single element or child component, for example to focus an input or call a child method.

## Know it

`@ViewChildren` queries the template and returns a `QueryList` of all matching elements, directives, or child components, not only the first.

---

## Explanation

The `QueryList` is live: it updates when matching items are added or removed. It also has a `changes` observable. It is useful when many similar children exist, such as the items of a list.

## Example

```text
export class TabsComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChildren(TabComponent) tabs!: QueryList<TabComponent>;

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    console.log('number of tabs:', this.tabs.length);
    this.tabs.forEach(tab => tab.reset());
  }
}
// template: <app-tab *ngFor="let t of items"></app-tab>
```

## When to use it

Use it when you need to work with a group of the same kind of child.

## Know it

With the Default strategy, Angular checks a component every time change detection runs, which happens after any event, timer, or HTTP response anywhere in the app.

---

## Explanation

It is simple and safe because the view always stays up to date, even if you mutate objects in place. The cost is that large apps check many components that did not change, which can slow things down.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-counter',
  template: `{{ count }}`
  // changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default   (this is the default)
})
export class CounterComponent {
  count = 0;
  inc() { this.count++; }   // view updates, even if mutated in place
}
```

## When to use it

It is fine for small apps and simple components. Switch to OnPush for large or frequently updated views.

## Know it

`ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` makes Angular check a component only when something relevant happens: an input reference changes, an event fires inside the component, an `async` pipe emits, a signal it reads changes, or `markForCheck()` is called.

---

## Explanation

It reduces unnecessary checks and speeds up large apps. The trade-off is that you must update data immutably: changing a property inside an object without replacing the object is not noticed by an OnPush child.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `{{ user.name }}`
})
export class UserComponent {
  @Input() user!: { name: string };
}

// parent: this.user.name = 'X'              -> child NOT updated (same reference)
// parent: this.user = { ...this.user, name: 'X' } -> child updates (new reference)
```

## When to use it

Use it for most components in larger apps, together with immutable data, the `async` pipe, and signals.

## Know it

`effect()` runs a function whenever the signals it reads change. It is meant for side effects such as logging, saving to storage, or calling non-Angular code.

---

## Explanation

An effect runs at least once, and runs again when any signal it read changes. It must be created in an injection context, for example in a constructor. Do not use it to compute values for the template; use `computed()` for that.

## Example

```text
export class SettingsComponent {
  theme = signal('light');

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      localStorage.setItem('theme', this.theme());   // runs when theme changes
    });
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for side effects that must follow signal changes, such as persisting state or syncing with a library.

## Know it

A route guard is a function (or class) that Angular runs during navigation to decide whether a route can be entered, left, or loaded. It can allow the navigation, block it, or redirect somewhere else.

---

## Explanation

Common guards are `canActivate` (can this route open?), `canDeactivate` (can the user leave?), and `canMatch` (should this route be considered at all?). Modern Angular uses functional guards. Guards improve the user experience, but real security must still be enforced on the server.

## Example

```text
export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return auth.isLoggedIn() ? true : router.parseUrl('/login');
};

// in the route config
{ path: 'admin', component: AdminComponent, canActivate: [authGuard] }
```

## When to use it

Use it to protect pages that need login or a permission, or to warn about unsaved changes.

## Know it

`track` is the built-in way to identify items in the newer `@for` control-flow block. It is required: you must write `track` followed by an expression such as `item.id`.

---

## Explanation

It does the same job as `trackBy` but with simpler syntax, and Angular warns you if you forget it. Use a unique, stable value like an id. `track $index` is acceptable only for static lists.

## Example

```text
@for (user of users; track user.id) {
  <li>{{ user.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>No users</li>
}
```

## When to use it

Use it in every `@for` loop, choosing a unique id as the tracking expression.

## Know it

A directive adds behavior to an existing element or changes the structure of the DOM, without having its own template. There are attribute directives and structural directives.

---

## Explanation

Attribute directives, such as `ngClass` or your own `appHighlight`, change how an element looks or behaves. Structural directives, such as `*ngIf` and `*ngFor`, add or remove elements. A component is simply a directive that also has a template.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appHighlight]', standalone: true })
export class HighlightDirective {
  constructor(el: ElementRef) {
    el.nativeElement.style.background = 'yellow';
  }
}

<p appHighlight>This paragraph gets a yellow background</p>
```

## When to use it

Use it for reusable behavior that applies to any element, such as highlighting, tooltips, or permission checks, with no template of its own.

## Know it

A pure pipe runs only when its input value changes (a new primitive or a new object reference). This is the default for pipes. Angular reuses the earlier result otherwise.

---

## Explanation

Because it is not recalculated on every change detection, a pure pipe is fast, and it is better than calling a method in a template. The catch: changing a property inside an object or pushing into an array does not trigger it, because the reference is the same.

## Example

```text
@Pipe({ name: 'initials', standalone: true })   // pure by default
export class InitialsPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(name: string) {
    return name.split(' ').map(p => p[0]).join('');
  }
}

<span>{{ 'Aniket Pardhi' | initials }}</span>   <!-- AP -->
```

## When to use it

Use pure pipes for formatting and simple transformations of immutable inputs.

## Know it

A hot Observable produces values independently of its subscribers and shares one source among them. A subscriber only receives values that are emitted after it subscribes.

---

## Explanation

A `Subject`, DOM events, and WebSocket messages are hot: they happen whether or not anyone is listening. A late subscriber misses earlier values, unless you add replay behavior with `ReplaySubject` or `shareReplay()`.

## Example

```ts
const hot = new Subject<string>();
hot.subscribe(v => console.log('A got', v));
hot.next('one');
hot.subscribe(v => console.log('B got', v));   // joins late
hot.next('two');
// A got one, A got two, B got two   (B missed 'one')
```

## When to use it

Use hot streams for events and shared sources that exist independently, like clicks, messages, and shared state.

## Know it

A `ReplaySubject` remembers a number of past values and replays them to every new subscriber. It does not need an initial value.

---

## Explanation

You choose how many values to remember, for example `new ReplaySubject(3)`. A subscriber that joins late receives those stored values first, then new ones. With no values emitted yet, it emits nothing, unlike a `BehaviorSubject`.

## Example

```ts
const replay = new ReplaySubject<number>(2);     // remember the last 2 values
replay.next(1);
replay.next(2);
replay.next(3);
replay.subscribe(v => console.log('late subscriber got', v));
// late subscriber got 2, late subscriber got 3
```

## When to use it

Use it when late subscribers need recent history, such as the last few messages or events.

## Know it

`switchMap` maps each value to an inner Observable and switches to the newest one, cancelling the previous inner Observable that is still running.

---

## Explanation

Only the result of the latest request is delivered, so older, slower responses can never overwrite newer ones. If the cancelled inner Observable is an HTTP request, the browser request is aborted.

## Example

```ts
const search = (term: string) => of('results for ' + term).pipe(delay(100));
from(['a', 'ab', 'abc']).pipe(
  concatMap(x => of(x).pipe(delay(30))),   // the user types every 30 ms
  switchMap(search)
).subscribe(r => console.log(r));
// printed: results for abc     (the earlier searches were cancelled)
```

## When to use it

Use it for search boxes, route parameters, and any case where only the latest request matters.

## Know it

`mergeMap` maps each value to an inner Observable and runs all of them at the same time, merging their results into one stream. Nothing is cancelled and the results can arrive in any order.

---

## Explanation

It is the right choice for independent work that should all finish, such as uploading several files. You can limit how many run at once with the `concurrent` argument, for example `mergeMap(fn, 3)`.

## Example

```ts
const search = (term: string) => of('results for ' + term).pipe(delay(100));
from(['a', 'ab', 'abc']).pipe(
  concatMap(x => of(x).pipe(delay(30))),
  mergeMap(search)
).subscribe(r => console.log(r));
// printed: results for a, results for ab, results for abc   (all of them)
```

## When to use it

Use it when every inner task matters and they may run in parallel, such as independent saves or uploads.

## Know it

`takeUntil(notifier$)` passes values through until the notifier Observable emits, and then completes the stream and unsubscribes.

---

## Explanation

The cut-off is decided by another Observable instead of a count. In Angular it was the standard way to stop subscriptions when a component is destroyed. It should be the last operator in the `pipe()` to avoid leaks, and `takeUntilDestroyed()` is its modern replacement for that purpose.

## Example

```ts
const stop$ = timer(350);
interval(100).pipe(takeUntil(stop$)).subscribe({
  next: n => console.log('tick', n),
  complete: () => console.log('stopped by the signal')
});
// tick 0, tick 1, tick 2, stopped by the signal
```

## When to use it

Use it to stop a stream when an event happens, such as a component being destroyed or a user pressing Cancel.

## Know it

`@if` is the built-in control-flow block that shows content only when a condition is true. It supports `@else if` and `@else`, and needs no import.

---

## Explanation

It was introduced in Angular 17 as a faster, easier replacement for `*ngIf`, with a cleaner syntax for the else part. You can also keep the result of the condition with `as`, for example `@if (user$ | async; as user)`.

## Example

```text
@if (user) {
  <p>Welcome {{ user.name }}</p>
} @else if (loading) {
  <p>Loading...</p>
} @else {
  <p>Please sign in</p>
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for all conditional content in new Angular code.

## Know it

`@for` is the built-in control-flow block that repeats a template for each item of a collection. It requires a `track` expression and supports an `@empty` block for empty lists.

---

## Explanation

`track` tells Angular how to identify each item, so it reuses DOM nodes when the list changes; use a unique id. Inside the loop you get variables such as `$index`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even`, `$odd`, and `$count`. It replaces `*ngFor`.

## Example

```text
@for (user of users; track user.id) {
  <li>{{ $index + 1 }}. {{ user.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>No users found</li>
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for every list in new templates, with a unique id as the tracking expression.

## Know it

`@HostListener` lets a directive or component listen to events on its own host element, or on `window` and `document`, from inside the class.

---

## Explanation

You give it the event name and optional arguments, and Angular calls the decorated method whenever the event fires. It cleans up the listener automatically when the component is destroyed, so you do not need `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener`.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appClickOutside]', standalone: true })
export class ClickOutsideDirective {
  @HostListener('document:click', ['$event'])
  onClick(event: MouseEvent) {
    console.log('clicked at', event.clientX, event.clientY);
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for host-level events such as keyboard shortcuts, window resize, and click-outside behavior.

## Know it

`@HostBinding` binds a class property to a property, attribute, class, or style of the host element, so the directive can change how its host element looks.

---

## Explanation

When the property changes, Angular updates the host automatically. It is the host-side equivalent of property binding. Newer code can use the `host` object in the decorator metadata for the same purpose.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appActive]', standalone: true })
export class ActiveDirective {
  @HostBinding('class.active') isActive = true;
  @HostBinding('attr.role') role = 'button';
}
// <div appActive>  ->  <div class="active" role="button">
```

## When to use it

Use it to add classes, styles, or attributes to the host element from a directive or component.

## Know it

`@HostListener` listens to events on the host element (view to class), while `@HostBinding` sets properties, classes, or styles on the host element (class to view).

---

## Explanation

Together they let a directive control its own host: one reacts to the user, the other changes the appearance. A common pattern is to toggle a property in a listener and let a binding show the result.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appHover]', standalone: true })
export class HoverDirective {
  @HostBinding('class.hovered') hovered = false;       // changes the host
  @HostListener('mouseenter') on()  { this.hovered = true; }   // listens to the host
  @HostListener('mouseleave') off() { this.hovered = false; }
}
```

## When to use it

Use both together for behavior directives such as hover effects, tooltips, and drag handles.

## Know it

`@ContentChild` queries the content that a parent projects into your component through `<ng-content>` and returns the first match. It reads projected content, not your own template.

---

## Explanation

The projected content is available from `ngAfterContentInit`. It is useful in reusable components such as tabs or cards that need to find an element or component supplied by the parent.

## Example

```text
@Component({ selector: 'app-panel', template: `<ng-content></ng-content>` })
export class PanelComponent implements AfterContentInit {
  @ContentChild('title') title!: ElementRef;
  ngAfterContentInit() { console.log(this.title.nativeElement.textContent); }
}

<app-panel><h3 #title>Settings</h3></app-panel>
```

## When to use it

Use it in wrapper components that need to inspect or control the content a parent passes in.

## Know it

`@ViewChild` finds elements in the component's own template, while `@ContentChild` finds elements that a parent projected into the component with `<ng-content>`.

---

## Explanation

The timing also differs: view queries are ready in `ngAfterViewInit`, content queries in `ngAfterContentInit` (which runs earlier). The plural versions, `@ViewChildren` and `@ContentChildren`, return all matches.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  template: `<h3 #own>Card</h3><ng-content></ng-content>`
})
export class CardComponent {
  @ViewChild('own') own!: ElementRef;          // from this template
  @ContentChild('given') given!: ElementRef;   // from the parent's projected content
}

<app-card><p #given>Passed in by the parent</p></app-card>
```

## When to use it

Use `ViewChild` for your own template and `ContentChild` for content supplied by the parent.

## Know it

`ElementRef` is a wrapper that gives you direct access to a native DOM element through its `nativeElement` property. You can inject it into a component or directive.

---

## Explanation

Using the native element lets you do anything the browser allows, but it ties your code to the browser and bypasses Angular's protections, so it can cause XSS risks and break server-side rendering. Prefer Angular bindings, and use `Renderer2` for DOM changes.

## Example

```text
@Component({ selector: 'app-search', template: `<input #box>` })
export class SearchComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChild('box') box!: ElementRef<HTMLInputElement>;
  ngAfterViewInit() {
    this.box.nativeElement.focus();      // direct DOM access
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it sparingly, for things bindings cannot do, like focusing an element or measuring its size.

## Know it

`Renderer2` is an Angular service for changing the DOM safely: setting styles, classes, attributes, and creating or removing elements, through an abstraction that works on every platform.

---

## Explanation

It works with the browser, with server-side rendering, and with web workers, because the actual DOM work is delegated to a platform renderer. It is the recommended alternative to touching `nativeElement` directly.

## Example

```text
constructor(private el: ElementRef, private renderer: Renderer2) {}

highlight() {
  this.renderer.setStyle(this.el.nativeElement, 'background', 'yellow');
  this.renderer.addClass(this.el.nativeElement, 'active');
}
```

## When to use it

Use it in directives and components whenever you must change the DOM from code.

## Know it

`ElementRef` gives you the raw native element, while `Renderer2` gives you methods to change it safely through Angular. Reading is done with `ElementRef`; modifying should be done with `Renderer2`.

---

## Explanation

Changing `nativeElement` directly works only in the browser and can bypass Angular's sanitization. `Renderer2` calls are platform-independent, so the same code works with server-side rendering. Often you use both: `ElementRef` to get the element and `Renderer2` to modify it.

## Example

```text
// risky: direct DOM access
this.el.nativeElement.style.color = 'red';
this.el.nativeElement.innerHTML = userText;     // XSS risk

// safe: through the renderer
this.renderer.setStyle(this.el.nativeElement, 'color', 'red');
this.renderer.setProperty(this.el.nativeElement, 'textContent', userText);
```

## When to use it

Prefer `Renderer2` for every DOM change, and use `ElementRef` only to obtain the element.

## Know it

`input()` declares a component input as a signal. The parent still binds with `[name]="value"`, but inside the component you read it by calling it, `name()`, and you can use it in `computed()` and `effect()`.

---

## Explanation

Signal inputs are read-only inside the component, can be required with `input.required<T>()`, and can transform values with `transform`. Because they are signals, derived values update automatically without `ngOnChanges`.

## Example

```text
@Component({ selector: 'app-price', standalone: true, template: `{{ total() }}` })
export class PriceComponent {
  price = input.required<number>();
  quantity = input(1);
  total = computed(() => this.price() * this.quantity());
}

<app-price [price]="20" [quantity]="3"></app-price>   <!-- 60 -->
```

## When to use it

Use it in new components, especially with `OnPush` and signals.

## Know it

`@Input()` is the decorator-based input, a plain property that changes over time. `input()` is the signal-based version: a read-only signal that you call as a function.

---

## Explanation

With `@Input` you detect changes through `ngOnChanges` or setters. With `input()` you simply use `computed()` or `effect()`, and Angular tracks the dependency for you. Signal inputs can be marked required and typed more strictly. Both bind the same way in the parent template.

## Example

```text
// decorator style
@Input() price = 0;
ngOnChanges() { this.total = this.price * 2; }

// signal style
price = input(0);
total = computed(() => this.price() * 2);      // updates automatically
```

## When to use it

Choose `input()` for new code; keep `@Input` in existing components until you migrate them.

## Know it

`inject()` is a function that gets a dependency from the current injector. It works in an injection context, such as a field initializer, a constructor, a factory, or a functional guard.

---

## Explanation

It is the modern alternative to constructor parameters. It allows functional guards, interceptors, and resolvers, works well with inheritance, and supports options such as `optional`, `self`, and `skipSelf`. Calling it outside an injection context throws an error.

## Example

```text
@Component({ selector: 'app-users', standalone: true, template: `...` })
export class UsersComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  private users = inject(UserService);
  list$ = this.users.getAll();
}

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => inject(AuthService).isLoggedIn();
```

## When to use it

Use it in new code, and always in functional guards, interceptors, and resolvers.

## Know it

Constructor injection asks for dependencies by listing them as constructor parameters with their types. Angular reads the types and supplies the matching instances.

---

## Explanation

It is the traditional way to use dependency injection, and the type of each parameter is the token. A parameter property such as `private http: HttpClient` declares and stores it in one step. It works only in classes created by Angular.

## Example

```text
export class OrderComponent {
  constructor(
    private http: HttpClient,
    private cart: CartService
  ) {}
}
```

## When to use it

It is perfectly valid and common in existing code; new code often prefers `inject()`.

## Know it

Both get a dependency from the injector. Constructor injection lists dependencies as parameters; `inject()` is a function call that can be used in field initializers and in functions that are not classes.

---

## Explanation

`inject()` avoids long constructors, makes subclasses simpler (no need to pass parameters to `super`), and is the only way to use dependencies in functional guards and interceptors. Constructor injection is slightly more explicit about what a class needs.

## Example

```text
// constructor
export class A { constructor(private http: HttpClient) {} }

// inject()
export class B { private http = inject(HttpClient); }

// only inject() works here (no class, no constructor)
export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) => {
  const token = inject(AuthService).token;
  return next(req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: 'Bearer ' + token } }));
};
```

## When to use it

Use `inject()` for new code and functional APIs; keep constructors where a codebase already uses them.

## Know it

`providers` makes a service available to the component's view children and to projected content children. `viewProviders` makes it available only to the view children.

---

## Explanation

The difference only matters when a component uses `<ng-content>`. With `providers`, a child passed in by a parent can still inject the service; with `viewProviders` it cannot, and will fall back to a higher injector or fail.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-a',
  providers: [Logger],      // visible to template children AND projected children
  template: `<ng-content></ng-content>`
})
export class AComponent {}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-b',
  viewProviders: [Logger],  // visible ONLY to template children
  template: `<ng-content></ng-content>`
})
export class BComponent {}
```

## When to use it

Use `providers` by default; use `viewProviders` only to keep a service away from projected content.

## Know it

Lifecycle hooks are special methods Angular calls automatically at set moments in the life of a component or directive, from creation to destruction.

---

## Explanation

The order is: constructor, ngOnChanges, ngOnInit, ngDoCheck, ngAfterContentInit, ngAfterContentChecked, ngAfterViewInit, ngAfterViewChecked, ngOnDestroy. Some run once (Init hooks, OnDestroy), others run on every change detection pass (DoCheck, Checked hooks).

## Example

```text
export class DemoComponent implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  constructor()  { console.log('1 constructor'); }
  ngOnInit()     { console.log('2 ngOnInit'); }
  ngOnDestroy()  { console.log('3 ngOnDestroy'); }
}
// Output order: 1 constructor -> 2 ngOnInit -> (component lives) -> 3 ngOnDestroy
```

## When to use it

Put each kind of work in the right hook: setup in ngOnInit, DOM access in ngAfterViewInit, cleanup in ngOnDestroy.

## Know it

`ngDoCheck` runs on every change detection pass, so you can write your own check for changes that Angular cannot see by itself.

---

## Explanation

Because it runs so often, anything heavy inside it slows the app. A typical use is spotting a change inside an object or array, where the reference stays the same.

## Example

```text
ngDoCheck() {
  if (this.items.length !== this.lastCount) {
    this.lastCount = this.items.length;
    console.log('items changed');
  }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it rarely, only when `ngOnChanges` cannot detect the change you care about.

## Know it

`ngOnDestroy` runs just before Angular removes a component. It is the place to clean up: unsubscribe, clear timers, and remove listeners.

---

## Explanation

If you forget cleanup, subscriptions and timers keep running after the component is gone. This is a memory leak, and it can cause duplicate API calls.

## Example

```text
sub!: Subscription;
timer: any;

ngOnInit() {
  this.sub = this.api.getData().subscribe();
  this.timer = setInterval(() => this.refresh(), 5000);
}
ngOnDestroy() {
  this.sub.unsubscribe();
  clearInterval(this.timer);
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for every subscription, timer, or manual listener that does not clean itself up.

## Know it

`NgZone` is the Angular service on top of Zone.js. It lets you run code inside or outside Angular's change detection.

---

## Explanation

Its two main methods are `runOutsideAngular()` (no change detection is triggered) and `run()` (go back inside and trigger it).

## Example

```text
constructor(private zone: NgZone) {}

start() {
  this.zone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
    window.addEventListener('mousemove', e => this.track(e));   // no change detection
  });
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for frequent events or third-party libraries that would otherwise trigger needless checks.

## Know it

`runOutsideAngular()` runs code without triggering change detection. `ngZone.run()` runs code inside the Angular zone so that the view updates afterwards.

---

## Explanation

They are used as a pair: do heavy or frequent work outside, then use `run()` only when you need to show a result.

## Example

```text
this.zone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
  setInterval(() => {
    this.counter++;                           // no UI refresh
    if (this.counter % 100 === 0) {
      this.zone.run(() => this.shown = this.counter);   // refresh now
    }
  }, 10);
});
```

## When to use it

Use the pair to keep animations, scroll tracking, and chart libraries from slowing the app.

## Know it

`detectChanges()` runs change detection on the component right now. `markForCheck()` only marks the component (and its parents) to be checked in the next normal pass.

---

## Explanation

`markForCheck` is the safe choice for OnPush components after async data arrives. `detectChanges` forces an immediate update and can be wasteful if overused.

## Example

```text
constructor(private cd: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

onData(res: Data) {
  this.data = res;
  this.cd.markForCheck();     // update at the next pass
  // this.cd.detectChanges(); // update right now
}
```

## When to use it

Use `markForCheck` for most OnPush cases; use `detectChanges` only when you need the DOM updated immediately.

## Know it

`trackBy` (or `track` in `@for`) tells Angular how to identify each item in a list. When the list changes, only the changed items are re-rendered.

---

## Explanation

Without it, a new array makes Angular destroy and recreate every DOM node. With a unique id, Angular reuses the nodes that match and updates only the difference.

## Example

```text
// old
<li *ngFor="let u of users; trackBy: trackById">{{ u.name }}</li>
trackById(i: number, u: User) { return u.id; }

// new
@for (u of users; track u.id) { <li>{{ u.name }}</li> }
```

## When to use it

Use it for every list that is reloaded or reordered, especially large ones.

## Know it

Reduce unneeded checks, load less code, and avoid repeated work: OnPush, trackBy, lazy loading, async pipe, pure pipes, caching, and proper unsubscribing.

---

## Explanation

Each item attacks a different cost: OnPush and signals cut change detection, lazy loading and `@defer` cut bundle size, trackBy cuts DOM work, `shareReplay` cuts API calls, and cleanup cuts memory use.

## Example

```text
@Component({ changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush })
@for (p of products; track p.id) { ... }
{ path: 'admin', loadChildren: () => import('./admin/routes') }
@defer (on viewport) { <app-heavy-chart /> }
products$ = this.http.get('/api/products').pipe(shareReplay(1));
```

## When to use it

Apply these as defaults on every project, then measure for the rest.

## Know it

`@defer` lazy-loads part of a template only when a trigger happens, such as entering the viewport, a click, or a timer.

---

## Explanation

The deferred components are moved into a separate bundle and downloaded only when needed. You can show `@placeholder`, `@loading`, and `@error` content in the meantime.

## Example

```text
@defer (on viewport) {
  <app-sales-chart />
} @placeholder {
  <p>Chart will load when visible</p>
} @loading {
  <p>Loading...</p>
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for heavy, below-the-fold parts such as charts, maps, and editors.

## Know it

Route guards are functions that decide if navigation to or away from a route is allowed. The main ones are `CanActivate`, `CanActivateChild`, `CanMatch`, `CanDeactivate`, and `Resolve`.

---

## Explanation

Each returns `true`, `false`, or a `UrlTree` (a redirect). Modern Angular uses functional guards with `inject()` instead of classes.

## Example

```text
export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return auth.isLoggedIn() ? true : router.createUrlTree(['/login']);
};

{ path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent, canActivate: [authGuard] }
```

## When to use it

Use them for login checks, role checks, unsaved-changes warnings, and data preloading.

## Know it

Store the user's role after login, then use a guard that checks the role before a route opens.

---

## Explanation

The flow is: login, get the role from the API or token, store it in an auth service, then a guard compares it with the role required by the route. Remember that frontend checks only improve the experience; the server must enforce permissions as well.

## Example

```text
export const roleGuard: CanActivateFn = (route) => {
  const required = route.data['role'];
  return inject(AuthService).role() === required || inject(Router).createUrlTree(['/forbidden']);
};

{ path: 'admin', component: AdminComponent, canActivate: [roleGuard], data: { role: 'Admin' } }
```

## When to use it

Use it for any app with different user types.

## Know it

A custom validator is a function that checks a control's value and returns an error object when it is invalid, or `null` when it is valid.

---

## Explanation

Use one when the built-in validators (`required`, `minLength`, `pattern`) are not enough. The error object key is what you check in the template.

## Example

```text
export function noSpaces(control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null {
  return /\s/.test(control.value) ? { hasSpaces: true } : null;
}

name = new FormControl('', [Validators.required, noSpaces]);
// 'a b'  -> errors: { hasSpaces: true }      'ab' -> null
```

## When to use it

Use it for business rules, such as a PAN number format or a banned word.

## Know it

`ControlValueAccessor` is the interface that lets a custom component work with `ngModel` and `formControlName` like a native input.

---

## Explanation

You implement `writeValue` (form to component), `registerOnChange` (component to form), `registerOnTouched`, and optionally `setDisabledState`. You also register the component with the `NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR` token, using `forwardRef`.

## Example

```text
@Component({
  selector: 'app-rating',
  providers: [{ provide: NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR, useExisting: forwardRef(() => RatingComponent), multi: true }],
  template: `<button *ngFor="let s of [1,2,3,4,5]" (click)="set(s)">{{ s <= value ? '★' : '☆' }}</button>`
})
export class RatingComponent implements ControlValueAccessor {
  value = 0; onChange = (v: number) => {}; onTouched = () => {};
  writeValue(v: number) { this.value = v; }
  registerOnChange(fn: any) { this.onChange = fn; }
  registerOnTouched(fn: any) { this.onTouched = fn; }
  set(v: number) { this.value = v; this.onChange(v); }
}
// <app-rating formControlName="rating"></app-rating>
```

## When to use it

Use it for custom inputs such as star ratings, date pickers, and tag inputs.

## Know it

Dynamic forms are built at runtime from a configuration, such as JSON from an API, instead of fixed HTML.

---

## Explanation

You loop over the config, create a `FormControl` for each field, and render the matching input in the template.

## Example

```text
fields = [
  { key: 'name', label: 'Name', required: true },
  { key: 'city', label: 'City', required: false }
];

form = new FormGroup(
  Object.fromEntries(this.fields.map(f =>
    [f.key, new FormControl('', f.required ? Validators.required : null)]))
);

@for (f of fields; track f.key) { <input [formControlName]="f.key" [placeholder]="f.label"> }
```

## When to use it

Use it when forms change often or differ per customer, such as in an ERP.

## Know it

The `async` pipe subscribes to an Observable or Promise in the template, shows the latest value, and unsubscribes automatically when the component is destroyed.

---

## Explanation

It also marks the component for checking when a new value arrives, so it works with OnPush. It removes the need for manual `subscribe` and `unsubscribe`.

## Example

```text
users$ = this.api.getUsers();

@for (u of users$ | async; track u.id) { <p>{{ u.name }}</p> }

@if (user$ | async; as user) { <h2>{{ user.name }}</h2> }
```

## When to use it

Use it as the default way to show Observable data.

## Know it

Use `@Directive` with a selector, then use `@HostListener` and `@HostBinding` to react to events and change the host element.

---

## Explanation

The selector is usually written in square brackets so it is used like an attribute. Inject `ElementRef` or use `@HostBinding` to change the element.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appHighlight]', standalone: true })
export class HighlightDirective {
  @HostBinding('style.background') bg = '';
  @HostListener('mouseenter') on()  { this.bg = 'yellow'; }
  @HostListener('mouseleave') off() { this.bg = ''; }
}
// <p appHighlight>Hover me</p>
```

## When to use it

Use it for reusable behavior such as highlighting, tooltips, and click-outside.

## Know it

`TemplateRef` is a reference to an `<ng-template>`, which is a blueprint. `ViewContainerRef` is a place in the DOM where Angular can add or remove views created from it.

---

## Explanation

Together they let a directive render a template when it wants. This is how `*ngIf` and `*ngFor` work internally. `ViewContainerRef.createComponent()` also creates components dynamically.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appIf]', standalone: true })
export class AppIfDirective {
  constructor(private tpl: TemplateRef<any>, private vcr: ViewContainerRef) {}
  @Input() set appIf(show: boolean) {
    this.vcr.clear();
    if (show) this.vcr.createEmbeddedView(this.tpl);
  }
}
// <p *appIf="visible">Shown only when visible is true</p>
```

## When to use it

Use them to build your own structural directives or load components dynamically.

## Know it

`HttpClient` is Angular's service for sending HTTP requests (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH). Each call returns an Observable.

---

## Explanation

The request is cold: it is sent only when you subscribe, and again for each subscription. You provide it with `provideHttpClient()`.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class UserService {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  getUsers()           { return this.http.get<User[]>('/api/users'); }
  addUser(u: User)     { return this.http.post<User>('/api/users', u); }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for every backend call, always inside a service.

## Know it

Use `catchError` in the service for specific cases, an interceptor for global cases such as 401 and 500, and show a clear message in the component.

---

## Explanation

`catchError` must return an Observable. Return `of(fallback)` to keep the app working, or `throwError` to pass the error on.

## Example

```text
getProducts() {
  return this.http.get<Product[]>('/api/products').pipe(
    catchError(err => {
      this.toast.show('Could not load products');
      return of([]);
    })
  );
}

// interceptor: on 401 -> router.navigate(['/login'])
```

## When to use it

Handle local cases in services; global cases in one interceptor.

## Know it

A wildcard route shows a 404 page for unknown URLs. For 500 errors, an interceptor can catch the failure and navigate to an error page.

---

## Explanation

404 is a routing problem, so the router handles it. 500 comes from the server, so you detect it in the HTTP layer.

## Example

```text
{ path: '**', component: NotFoundComponent }

export const errorInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =>
  next(req).pipe(catchError(err => {
    if (err.status >= 500) inject(Router).navigate(['/server-error']);
    return throwError(() => err);
  }));
```

## When to use it

Every production app needs both.

## Know it

`TestBed` builds a small Angular testing module so you can create components and inject services the way Angular does at runtime.

---

## Explanation

You configure imports and providers, then call `TestBed.createComponent()` or `TestBed.inject()`. Standalone components go in `imports`.

## Example

```text
beforeEach(() => {
  TestBed.configureTestingModule({
    imports: [UserCardComponent],
    providers: [UserService]
  });
});
const service = TestBed.inject(UserService);
```

## When to use it

Use it in almost every Angular unit test.

## Know it

Create it with `TestBed`, get the `ComponentFixture`, set inputs, call `fixture.detectChanges()`, then check the instance and the rendered HTML.

---

## Explanation

`fixture.componentInstance` is the class, and `fixture.nativeElement` is the DOM. `detectChanges()` is needed to update the view after you change data.

## Example

```text
let fixture: ComponentFixture<UserCardComponent>;

beforeEach(() => {
  TestBed.configureTestingModule({ imports: [UserCardComponent] });
  fixture = TestBed.createComponent(UserCardComponent);
});

it('shows the name', () => {
  fixture.componentInstance.user = { name: 'Ram' };
  fixture.detectChanges();
  expect(fixture.nativeElement.textContent).toContain('Ram');
});
```

## When to use it

Use it to check what the user sees and how the component reacts.

## Know it

Use `provideHttpClientTesting()` and `HttpTestingController` to catch requests and answer them with fake data, without a real server.

---

## Explanation

You call the service, then `expectOne(url)` to find the request, check its method, and `flush()` a fake response. `verify()` ensures no request was left unanswered.

## Example

```text
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [UserService, provideHttpClient(), provideHttpClientTesting()]
});
const http = TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController);

service.getUsers().subscribe(u => expect(u.length).toBe(2));
const req = http.expectOne('/api/users');
expect(req.request.method).toBe('GET');
req.flush([{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }]);
http.verify();
```

## When to use it

Use it to test every service method that calls an API.

## Know it

A spy replaces a real method with a fake one you control and can check. A mock is a fake object used instead of a real dependency.

---

## Explanation

They keep unit tests fast and focused: the component test does not need a real service or API. In Jasmine you use `createSpyObj`; in Jest you use `jest.fn()`.

## Example

```text
const spy = jasmine.createSpyObj('UserService', ['getUsers']);
spy.getUsers.and.returnValue(of([{ id: 1, name: 'Ram' }]));

TestBed.configureTestingModule({ providers: [{ provide: UserService, useValue: spy }] });
// later: expect(spy.getUsers).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
```

## When to use it

Mock every dependency that is not the thing under test.

## Know it

An `NgModule` groups components, directives, pipes, and services. A standalone component needs no module and lists its own imports.

---

## Explanation

Standalone is the modern default (Angular 17+). It means less boilerplate and easier lazy loading. Modules still exist in older projects.

## Example

```text
// module style
@NgModule({ declarations: [CardComponent], imports: [CommonModule], exports: [CardComponent] })
export class CardModule {}

// standalone style
@Component({ standalone: true, imports: [CommonModule], selector: 'app-card', template: '...' })
export class CardComponent {}
```

## When to use it

Standalone for new code; keep NgModules when maintaining older projects.

## Know it

Parent to child uses `@Input`, child to parent uses `@Output` with an event emitter, unrelated components use a shared service, and large apps use a state library or signals.

---

## Explanation

Choose by relationship: direct parent or child, siblings through the parent, anywhere through a service, or global state for big apps.

## Example

```text
// child
@Input() title = '';
@Output() saved = new EventEmitter<string>();
save() { this.saved.emit(this.title); }

// parent
<app-form [title]="t" (saved)="onSaved($event)"></app-form>
```

## When to use it

Inputs and outputs for nearby components; a service for distant ones.

## Know it

A smart (container) component handles data, API calls, and state. A dumb (presentational) component only shows data from inputs and sends events through outputs.

---

## Explanation

Dumb components are easy to reuse and test because they have no dependencies. Smart components connect them to services.

## Example

```text
// smart
users$ = this.api.getUsers();
<app-user-list [users]="users$ | async" (select)="open($event)"></app-user-list>

// dumb
@Input() users: User[] = [];
@Output() select = new EventEmitter<User>();
```

## When to use it

Split features this way to keep the UI simple and testable.

## Know it

Signals give Angular fine-grained reactivity: it knows which exact bindings depend on which values, so it updates only those, instead of checking whole components.

---

## Explanation

This means less change detection work, simpler state code, and a path to apps that run without Zone.js.

## Example

```text
count = signal(0);
// template: {{ count() }}
// when count changes, only this binding is refreshed, not the whole component tree
```

## When to use it

Mention it when asked why modern Angular is moving toward signals.

## Know it

`computed()` creates a read-only signal whose value is calculated from other signals and updates automatically when they change.

---

## Explanation

It is lazy and cached: the function runs only when the value is read and a dependency changed. You cannot set it directly.

## Example

```ts
function signal(v) { const f = () => v; f.set = x => { v = x; }; return f; }
function computed(fn) { return () => fn(); }

const price = signal(100);
const qty = signal(2);
const total = computed(() => price() * qty());

console.log(total());   // 200
qty.set(3);
console.log(total());   // 300
```

## When to use it

Use it for derived values such as totals, full names, and filtered lists.

## Know it

`effect()` runs a function automatically whenever the signals it reads change. It is for side effects, not for producing values.

---

## Explanation

Use it for things outside the template: logging, saving to `localStorage`, or calling a non-signal API. Do not use it to set other signals in a loop; use `computed()` for derived values.

## Example

```text
count = signal(0);

constructor() {
  effect(() => {
    localStorage.setItem('count', String(this.count()));   // runs on every change
  });
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for side effects that must follow a signal; otherwise prefer `computed()`.

## Know it

`resource()` and `httpResource()` load async data into signals and give you `value()`, `isLoading()`, and `error()` automatically. `httpResource` is built for HTTP calls.

---

## Explanation

The request re-runs when the signals it reads change, and an old request is cancelled. You do not write `subscribe`, loading flags, or error flags by hand.

## Example

```text
id = signal(1);
user = httpResource<User>(() => `/api/users/${this.id()}`);

@if (user.isLoading()) { <p>Loading...</p> }
@if (user.error()) { <p>Failed</p> }
@if (user.value(); as u) { <h2>{{ u.name }}</h2> }
```

## When to use it

Use it for simple data fetching that depends on signals.

## Know it

A Promise gives one value, starts immediately, and cannot be cancelled. An Observable can give many values over time, starts when subscribed, and can be cancelled.

---

## Explanation

That is why Angular uses Observables: HTTP calls can be cancelled with `switchMap`, events are streams, and operators let you transform data.

## Example

```rxjs
const promise = new Promise(res => { console.log('promise runs now'); res(1); });
const obs$ = new rxjs.Observable(s => { console.log('observable runs on subscribe'); s.next(1); s.next(2); s.complete(); });

console.log('before subscribe');
obs$.subscribe(v => console.log('got', v));
// promise runs now
// before subscribe
// observable runs on subscribe
// got 1
// got 2
```

## When to use it

Observables for streams, cancellation, and Angular APIs; Promises for simple one-time `async/await` work.

## Know it

`combineLatest` emits when any source emits. `forkJoin` emits once, after all sources complete. `zip` pairs values by position. `withLatestFrom` adds the latest value of another stream when the main stream emits.

---

## Explanation

Pick by question: do I need all final results (forkJoin), the latest of everything on any change (combineLatest), matching pairs (zip), or a snapshot at the time of an event (withLatestFrom)?

## Example

```rxjs
const { of, interval, combineLatest, forkJoin, zip } = rxjs;
const { take, map } = rxjs.operators;

const a$ = interval(100).pipe(take(3), map(i => 'a' + i));
const b$ = interval(150).pipe(take(2), map(i => 'b' + i));

combineLatest([a$, b$]).subscribe(v => console.log('combineLatest', v));
forkJoin([a$, b$]).subscribe(v => console.log('forkJoin', v));
zip(a$, b$).subscribe(v => console.log('zip', v));
```

## When to use it

forkJoin for page-load API calls; combineLatest for filters; zip rarely; withLatestFrom for a click that needs current form state.

## Know it

`takeUntil(destroy$)` needs your own Subject that you complete in `ngOnDestroy`. `takeUntilDestroyed()` does the same automatically, using Angular's `DestroyRef`.

---

## Explanation

The new way has less code and is harder to forget. In a constructor or field initializer you can call `takeUntilDestroyed()` with no argument; elsewhere pass a `DestroyRef`.

## Example

```text
// old
private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();
ngOnInit()    { this.api.getData().pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(); }
ngOnDestroy() { this.destroy$.next(); this.destroy$.complete(); }

// new
private destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);
ngOnInit() { this.api.getData().pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)).subscribe(); }
```

## When to use it

Use `takeUntilDestroyed` in Angular 16 and above.

## Know it

`tsconfig.json` tells the TypeScript compiler how to turn TypeScript into JavaScript: target version, module type, strictness, and path aliases.

---

## Explanation

Angular projects enable `strict` mode for safer code. You can also add `paths` aliases so imports are short.

## Example

```text
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "strict": true,
    "paths": { "@app/*": ["src/app/*"] }
  }
}
// import { UserService } from '@app/services/user.service';
```

## When to use it

Edit it to enable strict checks or add import aliases.

## Know it

Dynamic component loading creates a component at runtime, on a condition or user action, instead of writing it in the template.

---

## Explanation

You get a `ViewContainerRef`, then call `createComponent()`. It is how modals, popups, and dashboard widgets are built.

## Example

```text
@ViewChild('host', { read: ViewContainerRef }) host!: ViewContainerRef;

open() {
  this.host.clear();
  const ref = this.host.createComponent(LoginComponent);
  ref.setInput('title', 'Sign in');
}
// <ng-template #host></ng-template>
```

## When to use it

Use it for modals, role-based screens, and widget dashboards.

## Know it

Use a shared service that holds the data in a `BehaviorSubject` (or a signal). Any component injects the service and reads it.

---

## Explanation

Components that are not parent and child cannot use `@Input` and `@Output`. A root-provided service is a single shared instance, so it works as the common place.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CartService {
  private count = new BehaviorSubject<number>(0);
  count$ = this.count.asObservable();
  add() { this.count.next(this.count.value + 1); }
}
// Header:  count$ | async     Product page: cart.add()
```

## When to use it

Use it for anything shared across screens: user, theme, cart, notifications.

## Know it

NgRx is a library that keeps the whole app state in one central store. Components read data from the store and send actions to change it.

---

## Explanation

The flow is: component dispatches an action, a reducer creates the new state, a selector reads part of it, and effects handle side effects like API calls. State is immutable and changes are traceable.

## Example

```text
// action
export const addItem = createAction('[Cart] Add', props<{ id: number }>());
// reducer
export const cartReducer = createReducer([], on(addItem, (s, { id }) => [...s, id]));
// component
this.store.dispatch(addItem({ id: 5 }));
items$ = this.store.select(selectCartItems);
```

## When to use it

Use it in large apps with complex shared state; avoid it for simple apps.

## Know it

Smart components fetch data and hold state. Dumb components only show inputs and emit events. The split keeps UI parts reusable and logic in one place.

---

## Explanation

A dumb component can be tested by just giving it inputs. A smart component wires services to dumb ones. This also works well with OnPush.

## Example

```text
// smart (page)
<app-user-table [users]="users$ | async" (delete)="remove($event)"></app-user-table>

// dumb (table)
@Input() users: User[] = [];
@Output() delete = new EventEmitter<number>();
```

## When to use it

Use it for every feature page with a list, form, or table.

## Know it

An `AsyncSubject` emits only its last value, and only when it completes. Every subscriber, even a late one, gets that final value.

---

## Explanation

Everything before the last value is ignored. It is like a Promise that resolves once with the final result.

## Example

```rxjs
const a = new rxjs.AsyncSubject();
a.subscribe(v => console.log('early', v));
a.next('10%'); a.next('60%'); a.next('Done');
a.complete();
a.subscribe(v => console.log('late', v));
// early Done, late Done
```

## When to use it

Use it when only the final result of a process matters.

## Know it

`debounceTime` waits until the user stops, then emits the last value. `throttleTime` emits the first value right away, then blocks for a fixed time.

---

## Explanation

Debounce suits search boxes: wait for the pause. Throttle suits scrolling: act regularly while it keeps happening.

## Example

```rxjs
const { Subject } = rxjs;
const { debounceTime, throttleTime } = rxjs.operators;

const s = new Subject();
s.pipe(debounceTime(200)).subscribe(v => console.log('debounce', v));
s.pipe(throttleTime(200)).subscribe(v => console.log('throttle', v));

['a', 'b', 'c'].forEach(v => s.next(v));
// throttle a   (immediately)
// debounce c   (after 200ms of quiet)
```

## When to use it

Debounce for typing; throttle for continuous events.

## Know it

`withLatestFrom` adds the latest value of another Observable each time the main Observable emits. The other stream never triggers an emission by itself.

---

## Explanation

If the other stream has not emitted yet, the main value is dropped. It is perfect for "when X happens, use the current value of Y".

## Example

```rxjs
const { Subject, BehaviorSubject } = rxjs;
const { withLatestFrom } = rxjs.operators;

const click$ = new Subject();
const username$ = new BehaviorSubject('Ram');

click$.pipe(withLatestFrom(username$)).subscribe(([_, name]) => console.log('Login with', name));
username$.next('Saurabh');
username$.next('John');
click$.next();            // Login with John
```

## When to use it

Use it to read current form or state values when a button is clicked.

## Know it

I would handle it in one HTTP interceptor. On the first 401, start a single refresh request and make every other failed request wait for that same refresh, then retry with the new token.

---

## Explanation

Use a flag (or a `BehaviorSubject`) that says "refresh in progress". The first 401 starts the refresh. Other 401s see the flag and wait until the new token is published, then retry. If the refresh itself fails, log the user out once.

## Example

```text
private refreshing = false;
private token$ = new BehaviorSubject<string | null>(null);

intercept(req, next) {
  return next.handle(this.addToken(req)).pipe(
    catchError(err => {
      if (err.status !== 401) return throwError(() => err);
      if (!this.refreshing) {
        this.refreshing = true;
        this.token$.next(null);
        return this.auth.refresh().pipe(
          switchMap(t => { this.refreshing = false; this.token$.next(t); return next.handle(this.addToken(req, t)); }),
          catchError(e => { this.refreshing = false; this.auth.logout(); return throwError(() => e); })
        );
      }
      return this.token$.pipe(filter(t => !!t), take(1), switchMap(t => next.handle(this.addToken(req, t!))));
    })
  );
}
```

## When to use it

Use this for any app with short-lived access tokens and a refresh token.

## Know it

It means a value used in the template changed after Angular had already checked it in the same change detection pass. It shows up in development mode only, but it points to a real design problem.

---

## Explanation

The usual cause is a child or a lifecycle hook (`ngAfterViewInit`, `ngAfterContentInit`) changing a parent-bound value during the pass. Fixes: set the value earlier (`ngOnInit`), move the logic so data flows one way (parent to child), use `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` after the change, or use a signal. Avoid `setTimeout` hacks unless nothing else fits.

## Example

```text
// problem: changes a bound value after the view was checked
ngAfterViewInit() { this.title = this.child.title; }

// fix 1: derive it earlier or pass it down as an input
// fix 2: tell Angular to check again
ngAfterViewInit() { this.title = this.child.title; this.cd.detectChanges(); }
```

## When to use it

Use this answer for any "ExpressionChanged" question; say the root cause is a value changing during change detection.

## Know it

I would reduce how much work happens on every keystroke: use `updateOn: 'blur'`, OnPush, split the form into smaller child components, avoid heavy template calls, and debounce expensive `valueChanges` work.

---

## Explanation

Every keystroke triggers validation and change detection. Splitting into child components with OnPush means only the changed section is checked. `updateOn: 'blur'` or `'submit'` makes validation run less often. Use `trackBy` for repeated rows, and avoid methods in the template.

## Example

```text
form = this.fb.group({
  name: ['', { validators: [Validators.required], updateOn: 'blur' }]
}, { updateOn: 'change' });

// split: <app-address-section [group]="form.controls.address"></app-address-section>
@Component({ changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush })

this.form.valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(300)).subscribe(v => this.saveDraft(v));
```

## When to use it

Use it for long forms, such as ERP entry screens.

## Know it

I would take heap snapshots before and after navigating, look for objects that should have been destroyed, and then check for subscriptions, timers, and listeners that are not cleaned up.

---

## Explanation

In Chrome DevTools, use the Memory tab: take a snapshot, navigate away and back several times, take another, and compare. Detached DOM nodes and growing component instances point to leaks. Common causes are `subscribe` without cleanup, `setInterval`, `addEventListener` on `window`, and services holding references to components.

## Example

```text
ngOnInit() {
  interval(5000).pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)).subscribe(() => this.refresh());
  fromEvent(window, 'resize').pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)).subscribe();
}
// or: use the async pipe and toSignal so cleanup is automatic
```

## When to use it

Use it for "the app gets slower the longer it stays open" questions.

## Know it

I would protect routes with a guard and hide UI with a small structural directive (or `@if` with a permission signal), driven by one auth service. The server must still enforce permissions.

---

## Explanation

Hiding a button only improves the experience; a user can still call the API. The guard handles navigation, the directive handles visibility, and the backend checks every request.

## Example

```text
@Directive({ selector: '[appHasRole]', standalone: true })
export class HasRoleDirective {
  private tpl = inject(TemplateRef); private vcr = inject(ViewContainerRef); private auth = inject(AuthService);
  @Input() set appHasRole(roles: string[]) {
    this.vcr.clear();
    if (roles.includes(this.auth.role())) this.vcr.createEmbeddedView(this.tpl);
  }
}
// <button *appHasRole="['Admin']">Delete</button>
```

## When to use it

Use it for any app with more than one type of user.

## Know it

I would use a WebSocket (or Server-Sent Events) wrapped in an RxJS stream, share it with `shareReplay`, reconnect on failure, and run high-frequency work outside Angular's zone.

---

## Explanation

`webSocket()` from RxJS gives an Observable. Use `retry` with a delay for reconnecting, and `share` so one connection serves many subscribers. If messages arrive many times per second, throttle them or update a signal only when needed.

## Example

```text
private socket$ = webSocket<Price>('wss://example.com/prices');

prices$ = this.socket$.pipe(
  retry({ delay: 3000 }),
  share()
);
// component: prices = toSignal(this.prices$);
```

## When to use it

Use it for dashboards, chat, order status, and notifications.

## Know it

Send the request with `reportProgress: true` and `observe: 'events'`, then read `HttpEventType.UploadProgress` events to update a progress value.

---

## Explanation

The Observable emits progress events and finally the response. Map them to a percentage, show it in the UI, and handle cancellation by unsubscribing.

## Example

```text
upload(file: File) {
  const body = new FormData(); body.append('file', file);
  this.http.post('/api/upload', body, { reportProgress: true, observe: 'events' }).subscribe(e => {
    if (e.type === HttpEventType.UploadProgress && e.total) this.progress.set(Math.round(100 * e.loaded / e.total));
    if (e.type === HttpEventType.Response) this.done.set(true);
  });
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for uploads of documents, images, and bulk files.

## Know it

Put the call in one service and cache it with `shareReplay(1)`, or store the result in a signal or `BehaviorSubject`, so every component reuses the same data.

---

## Explanation

HTTP observables are cold, so each subscriber makes its own request. Sharing the stream turns that into one request. Add a way to refresh the cache when the data changes.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class MasterService {
  private refresh$ = new BehaviorSubject<void>(undefined);
  countries$ = this.refresh$.pipe(
    switchMap(() => this.http.get<Country[]>('/api/countries')),
    shareReplay(1)
  );
  reload() { this.refresh$.next(); }
}
```

## When to use it

Use it for lookup data that rarely changes: countries, categories, user profile.

## Know it

Provide a custom `ErrorHandler` for unexpected runtime errors, and an HTTP interceptor for failed API calls. Log both to a monitoring service and show a friendly message.

---

## Explanation

`ErrorHandler` catches errors thrown in components and services that nobody handled. The interceptor catches HTTP failures. Together they cover most cases without try/catch everywhere.

## Example

```text
@Injectable()
export class GlobalErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  private toast = inject(ToastService);
  handleError(error: unknown) {
    console.error(error);                       // or send to a logging service
    this.toast.show('Something went wrong');
  }
}
providers: [{ provide: ErrorHandler, useClass: GlobalErrorHandler }]
```

## When to use it

Use it in every production app.

## Know it

Load a config file such as `assets/config.json` at startup with `provideAppInitializer` (or `APP_INITIALIZER`), store it in a service, and read the URL from there instead of from `environment.ts`.

---

## Explanation

`environment.ts` is baked into the build, so changing it needs a rebuild. A JSON file in assets can be replaced by DevOps per environment. The initializer makes Angular wait until the config is loaded before the app starts.

## Example

```text
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ConfigService {
  config!: { apiUrl: string };
  load() { return firstValueFrom(this.http.get<{ apiUrl: string }>('assets/config.json')).then(c => this.config = c); }
}
provideAppInitializer(() => inject(ConfigService).load())
```

## When to use it

Use it when the same build must run in several environments.

## Know it

The code runs on the server, where `window` and `document` do not exist. I would avoid direct browser globals, inject `DOCUMENT`, and run browser-only code only when `isPlatformBrowser` is true, or inside `afterNextRender`.

---

## Explanation

Prefer Angular abstractions: `inject(DOCUMENT)`, `Renderer2`, and `afterNextRender()`. For libraries that need the browser, load them only on the client.

## Example

```text
private platformId = inject(PLATFORM_ID);

ngOnInit() {
  if (isPlatformBrowser(this.platformId)) {
    localStorage.setItem('visited', 'true');
  }
}
// or:  afterNextRender(() => initChart());
```

## When to use it

Use it whenever an SSR or prerendering build breaks on browser APIs.

## Know it

Replace the service with a mock or spy through the testing module, so the test runs without a real API, and check the component's own behavior.

---

## Explanation

Provide the service with `useValue` pointing at a fake that returns `of(data)`. Call `fixture.detectChanges()` to run `ngOnInit`, then assert on the component and the rendered HTML. Test the service separately with `HttpTestingController`.

## Example

```text
const api = jasmine.createSpyObj('UserService', ['getUsers']);
api.getUsers.and.returnValue(of([{ id: 1, name: 'Ram' }]));

TestBed.configureTestingModule({ imports: [UsersComponent], providers: [{ provide: UserService, useValue: api }] });
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(UsersComponent);
fixture.detectChanges();
expect(fixture.nativeElement.textContent).toContain('Ram');
```

## When to use it

Use it for every component that depends on a service.

## Know it

Use `exhaustMap` on the click stream so clicks are ignored while a request is running, and also disable the button using a loading flag.

---

## Explanation

Disabling the button helps the user see what is happening. `exhaustMap` protects the logic even if the button is not disabled, for example from a keyboard shortcut.

## Example

```text
clicks$ = new Subject<void>();
saving = signal(false);

this.clicks$.pipe(
  exhaustMap(() => {
    this.saving.set(true);
    return this.api.save().pipe(finalize(() => this.saving.set(false)));
  })
).subscribe();
```

## When to use it

Use it for submit, pay, and create actions.

## Know it

A debounce wraps a function and delays calling it until no new call has arrived for a set time. Each new call clears the previous timer.

---

## Explanation

The wrapper keeps a timer in a closure. Every call resets it, so only the last call in a burst runs. This is the same idea as RxJS `debounceTime`.

## Example

```js
function debounce(fn, ms) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}

const search = debounce(term => console.log('search for', term), 100);
search('a'); search('an'); search('ang');   // typed quickly
// after 100ms prints once:  search for ang
```

## When to use it

Use it for search boxes, resize handlers, and autosave.

## Know it

Use event delegation: add one listener to the parent and check `event.target` to find which child was clicked.

---

## Explanation

Events bubble up from the clicked element to its parents. One listener on the parent catches them all, and it also works for items added later.

## Example

```js
// simulated bubbling so it runs without a browser
const handlers = [];
const parent = { addEventListener: (t, h) => handlers.push(h) };
parent.addEventListener('click', e => {
  if (e.target.matches('button')) console.log('clicked', e.target.id);
});
const click = id => handlers.forEach(h => h({ target: { id, matches: s => s === 'button' } }));
click('save'); click('delete');
// clicked save, clicked delete
```

## When to use it

Use it for long lists, tables, and dynamic content.

## Know it

Process the ids in batches: run each batch with `Promise.all`, wait for it, then start the next. This limits parallel requests.

---

## Explanation

`Promise.all` on all 20 would send 20 at once. Splitting into chunks keeps the load on the server and the browser under control. In RxJS you could use `mergeMap` with a concurrency number.

## Example

```js
const fakeApi = id => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('result ' + id), 20));

async function inBatches(ids, size) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i += size) {
    const batch = ids.slice(i, i + size);
    out.push(...await Promise.all(batch.map(fakeApi)));
    console.log('finished batch', i / size + 1);
  }
  return out;
}
inBatches([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], 3).then(r => console.log(r.length, 'results'));
// finished batch 1, 2, 3  then  7 results
```

## When to use it

Use it for bulk uploads and mass updates.

## Know it

Use memoization: store results in a cache keyed by the arguments, and return the cached value when the same arguments come again.

---

## Explanation

It only works for pure functions (same input, same output, no side effects). The cache uses memory, so limit its size for large inputs.

## Example

```js
function memoize(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return n => {
    if (cache.has(n)) { console.log('cache hit', n); return cache.get(n); }
    const v = fn(n); cache.set(n, v); return v;
  };
}
const slowSquare = n => n * n;
const fast = memoize(slowSquare);
console.log(fast(9));   // 81
console.log(fast(9));   // cache hit 9, then 81
```

## When to use it

Use it for heavy calculations, selectors, and recursive functions such as Fibonacci.

## Know it

JavaScript runs on one thread, so a long loop blocks everything. I would split the work into small chunks that yield to the event loop, or move it to a Web Worker.

---

## Explanation

Chunking with `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback` lets the browser repaint and handle clicks between chunks. A Web Worker runs the work on another thread entirely, so the UI never waits.

## Example

```js
function processInChunks(items, chunkSize, done) {
  let i = 0;
  (function next() {
    const end = Math.min(i + chunkSize, items.length);
    for (; i < end; i++) { /* heavy work on items[i] */ }
    if (i < items.length) setTimeout(next, 0);   // let the browser breathe
    else done();
  })();
}
processInChunks(new Array(1000).fill(0), 250, () => console.log('all items processed'));
```

## When to use it

Use it for large imports, big calculations, and report building in the browser.

## Know it

Use an `AbortController`: start a timer that calls `abort()`, and pass the controller's `signal` to `fetch`. The promise rejects with an `AbortError`.

---

## Explanation

Promises cannot be cancelled by themselves, but `fetch` supports `AbortSignal`. The same controller can also cancel the request when the user leaves the page.

## Example

```js
// a fake fetch that respects an abort signal, so this runs anywhere
const fakeFetch = (url, { signal }) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  const t = setTimeout(() => resolve('data'), 500);
  signal.addEventListener('abort', () => { clearTimeout(t); reject(new Error('AbortError')); });
});

const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 100);               // timeout after 100ms
fakeFetch('/api/slow', { signal: controller.signal })
  .then(console.log)
  .catch(e => console.log('failed:', e.message));
// failed: AbortError
```

## When to use it

Use it for slow networks, search-as-you-type, and component cleanup.

## Know it

Use `Promise.allSettled`. It waits for every promise and tells you which ones succeeded and which failed.

---

## Explanation

`Promise.all` rejects as soon as one promise rejects. `allSettled` returns an array of `{ status, value }` or `{ status, reason }`, so you can use the good results and report the failures.

## Example

```js
const ok = Promise.resolve('users');
const bad = Promise.reject(new Error('orders failed'));

Promise.allSettled([ok, bad]).then(results => {
  results.forEach(r => console.log(r.status, r.status === 'fulfilled' ? r.value : r.reason.message));
});
// fulfilled users
// rejected orders failed
```

## When to use it

Use it for dashboards where partial data is better than none.

## Know it

Wrap it in a function that keeps a flag (or the result) in a closure and ignores later calls.

---

## Explanation

The closure remembers whether the function has already run. This is useful for initialization that must happen one time, and it shows closures in a practical way.

## Example

```js
function once(fn) {
  let called = false, result;
  return (...args) => {
    if (!called) { called = true; result = fn(...args); }
    return result;
  };
}

const init = once(() => { console.log('initialized'); return 42; });
console.log(init());   // initialized, 42
console.log(init());   // 42  (does not run again)
```

## When to use it

Use it for setup code, one-time subscriptions, and payment-submit guards.

## Know it

Use a discriminated union: each state has a `status` field with a literal value, and only the data that belongs to that state.

---

## Explanation

TypeScript narrows the type when you check `status`, so you can only read `data` in the success case. This removes "loading and error at the same time" bugs.

## Example

```ts
type State =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: string[] }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };

function render(s: State) {
  switch (s.status) {
    case 'loading': return 'Loading...';
    case 'success': return s.data.join(', ');
    case 'error':   return 'Failed: ' + s.message;
  }
}
console.log(render({ status: 'success', data: ['a', 'b'] }));   // a, b
console.log(render({ status: 'error', message: 'timeout' }));   // Failed: timeout
```

## When to use it

Use it for API state, form steps, and any value that is in exactly one of several modes.

## Know it

Type it as `unknown` and narrow it with a type guard before use. Do not cast with `as` unless you have already checked it.

---

## Explanation

`any` turns off type checking, but `unknown` forces you to prove the type first. A type guard function returns `value is User`, and TypeScript trusts it inside the `if`.

## Example

```ts
interface User { id: number; name: string }

function isUser(v: unknown): v is User {
  return typeof v === 'object' && v !== null &&
    typeof (v as any).id === 'number' && typeof (v as any).name === 'string';
}

const data: unknown = JSON.parse('{"id":1,"name":"Ram"}');
if (isUser(data)) console.log(data.name.toUpperCase());   // RAM
else console.log('not a user');
```

## When to use it

Use it for API responses, local storage, and anything from outside your code.

## Know it

Use the `satisfies` operator. It checks the object against the type, but keeps the more precise inferred type instead of widening it.

---

## Explanation

A type annotation widens `'admin'` to `string`. `satisfies` validates the shape and keeps `'admin'` as a literal, so later code can rely on exact values.

## Example

```ts
type Routes = Record<string, { path: string }>;

const routes = {
  home: { path: '/home' },
  admin: { path: '/admin' }
} satisfies Routes;

console.log(routes.admin.path);     // /admin
// routes.missing                    // compile error: key does not exist (an annotation would allow it)
```

## When to use it

Use it for config maps, route tables, and theme objects.

## Know it

Use function overloads: write several signatures describing the allowed input and output pairs, then one implementation.

---

## Explanation

Overloads give callers precise return types. The implementation signature is not visible to callers, so it must handle every overload.

## Example

```ts
function format(value: string): string;
function format(value: number): string;
function format(value: Date): string;
function format(value: string | number | Date): string {
  if (value instanceof Date) return value.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
  if (typeof value === 'number') return value.toFixed(2);
  return value.trim();
}
console.log(format(5));              // 5.00
console.log(format('  hi '));        // hi
console.log(format(new Date(0)));    // 1970-01-01
```

## When to use it

Use it for utility functions with a few well-defined input shapes; a union is simpler if the return type does not change.

## Know it

Use branded types: add a fake, compile-time-only tag to the number type so the compiler treats the two ids as different.

---

## Explanation

Structurally both are `number`, so TypeScript would accept either. A brand (`number & { __brand: 'UserId' }`) makes them incompatible. It costs nothing at runtime because the brand does not exist in JavaScript.

## Example

```ts
type UserId = number & { __brand: 'UserId' };
type OrderId = number & { __brand: 'OrderId' };

const userId = 5 as UserId;
const orderId = 5 as OrderId;

function getOrder(id: OrderId) { return 'order ' + id; }
console.log(getOrder(orderId));     // order 5
// getOrder(userId);                 // compile error: UserId is not OrderId
```

## When to use it

Use it for ids, currencies, and units where mixing values causes real bugs.

## Know it

Use `Record<Union, ValueType>`. TypeScript then reports an error if any key of the union is missing.

---

## Explanation

`Record<K, V>` builds an object type with keys `K`. When `K` is a union, adding a new member to the union breaks every `Record` that forgot it, which is exactly the safety you want.

## Example

```ts
type Status = 'pending' | 'success' | 'failed';

const label: Record<Status, string> = {
  pending: 'Waiting',
  success: 'Done',
  failed: 'Error'
  // removing one of these gives a compile error
};
const show = (s: Status) => label[s];
console.log(show('success'));        // Done
```

## When to use it

Use it for lookup tables such as labels, colors, and icons per status.

